From 649a222d8d308c0097e1625f064e7f2f044637e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim <107831991+SyntaxError-PEBKAC@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 15:06:27 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 20/22] ducksteps: replace the About dialog's flat purple with
 a space backdrop

The dialog's background was #20123a, which is upstream's colour and was
never ours. The icon is a circular scene of deep navy space with a
sunset in it, so against flat purple its edge read as a hard disc pasted
onto an unrelated field.

about-background.svg is drawn from the icon's own palette, sampled from
the artwork rather than guessed: navy #000d24, indigo #17173d, the
sunset ramp #fe6405 / #fefe9c, swoosh silver #c1c4c0 and rust #983429.
A warm bloom sits under the icon so the sunset inside it carries out
into the dialog, which is what does most of the blending. Beyond that:
a spiral galaxy above the icon, an emission nebula in the top-right
corner, three comets, and four-point glints cut to the same shape as
the stars drawn inside the icon.

Everything with detail in it is placed clear of the text columns - the
logo occupies x 40-232, the wordmark x 275-474, the copy x 273-703 - and
a soft scrim holds the middle back so the copy keeps its contrast.

The stars twinkle and the galaxy's core fades in and out. Both live
inside the SVG behind @media not (prefers-reduced-motion), which is the
same shape as toolkit/themes/shared/icons/loading.svg, an animated SVG
this dialog already loads as an image. The brightness tiers are declared
outside that query on purpose: an animation outranks a normal
declaration while it runs, so those values are what remain when motion
is off. Without them the reduced-motion still frame would render every
glint at full opacity.

background-color stays set, to the artwork's own deepest navy, so a
dialog painted before the image decodes looks like the artwork rather
than like white. The whole thing stays inside upstream's
prefers-contrast guard, so a light high-contrast desktop is untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
